sources.app.tsx:
// A desktop from 2000 around the scene it wraps: the scene in a window with a
// yellow tab, the Deskbar in the top right corner and a few icons under it. The
// Deskbar's gem opens the scene list, its tray (signal, battery, clock) the frame's
// page, and below them the running scene puts its window away and brings it back,
// and Settings opens its settings. The tab's close box closes the scene to the
// list, its zoom box fills the desktop with the window and back, and a double tap
// on the tab hides the window. On a narrow panel the Deskbar runs along the top,
// with no room for the icons. Built-in scenes are drawn in the window too.
//
// On a frame that runs its scenes side by side (a Raspberry Pi: app.frame.scenes
// is there), it is a desktop: every scene opened is a window, and they all run at
// once, and chosen in another desktop's place (the frame page's Layout), it opens what
// that one had open. A window opens at the layout's "Window size" (800x480, a 7.5-inch frame's,
// unless it says otherwise), filling the desktop beside the Deskbar where that does
// not fit, and where it was when its scene opens again. The scene with the focus is
// the frame's own (the <yield />, its tab yellow), the others are <Scene>s (their
// tabs grey). Drag a tab to move a window and its frame to size it, a double click
// on the tab puts it away, the zoom box fills the desktop with it, and let go at
// the top edge it fills it, at a side it takes that half. The Deskbar lists the
// windows open (a click brings one forward, or puts the one in front away), its gem
// opens the Be menu (the frame's scenes, the scene store and the asset browser
// where the frame has them, Preferences, Terminal), and the icons under it (a
// double click) the frame's own apps: Frame (Preferences), Scenes (the scene
// list), those two and Terminal. F11 or the Be
// menu's "Full screen" gives the panel to the window in front (Escape takes it
// back, and the pointer at the top edge brings the Deskbar). Alt+Tab goes between
// windows, Alt+W closes the one in front. The gear box on a scene's tab opens its
// settings ("Clock Settings", a window a scene, several open at once); the frame's
// own scenes open in windows of their own over the scene, stay open while another
// window has the focus, and close when they go back by themselves (Save, Cancel, a
// scene picked).
const pad = (n) => String(n).padStart(2, '0')
const on = (value) => value === true || value === 'true'

// The frame's own scenes (the contract's `scenes.settings`, `scenes.switcher` and
// `scenes.terminal`), and the command that opens each, which also sends it back.
const SETTINGS = 'system/sceneSettings'
// On a desktop every scene's settings are a scene of their own, this and the
// scene's id (the contract's `scenes.settingsWindow`); the plain id is the frame's
// page, Preferences.
const SETTINGS_OF = 'system/sceneSettings#'
const settingsOf = (id) => SETTINGS_OF + id
const settingsFor = (id) => (typeof id === 'string' && id.startsWith(SETTINGS_OF) ? id.slice(SETTINGS_OF.length) : null)
const SWITCHER = 'system/sceneSwitcher'
const TERMINAL = 'system/terminal'
const commandOf = (id) => (id === SWITCHER ? 'sceneSwitcher' : id === TERMINAL ? 'terminal' : 'sceneSettings')
const isSystem = (id) => typeof id === 'string' && id.startsWith('system/')
// The frame's own apps that are scenes like any other, which the frame may have
// (docs/scene-store.md): the scene store and the asset browser, by their sample's
// id, or by their name where a control plane gave them another id. A desktop has
// entries of its own for them, and leaves them out of its scene lists.
const appIs = (id, name) => (scene) =>
  !!scene && (scene.id === id || String(scene.name || '').trim().toLowerCase() === name)
const isStore = appIs('scene-store', 'scene store')
const isAssets = appIs('asset-browser', 'asset browser')
const isFrameApp = (scene) => isStore(scene) || isAssets(scene)
